  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nWidespread light rain will continue early this morning as a low\npressure system over northern Illinois tracks through the northern\nOhio Valley. This rain will end from west to east throughout the\nmorning as the low pulls away from the region. A few rumbles of\nthunder cannot be ruled out, as GLM and lightning data indicate a\nfew flashes at various times. Additional showers and a few \nthunderstorms will be possible this afternoon from daytime heating\nas some modest instability builds across the region with a few\nhundred J/kg of SBCAPE. Highs today will generally range from the\nmiddle 70s to around 80 under cloud cover and persistent \nnortheast winds.\n\nSurface high pressure will build into the Upper Great Lakes\ntonight and remain dominant through Monday night. This will yield\nclear to partly cloudy skies, light winds, and pleasant mid-\nsummer conditions. High temperatures will steadily warm from the \nmid and upper 70s on Sunday to the upper 70s to lower 80s on \nMonday under ample sunshine.\n\nThe next notable weather system arrives Tuesday into Wednesday as\nan upper-level shortwave trough and associated surface cold front\npush east across the Northern Plains into the Upper Midwest.\nMoisture return ahead of the boundary will push dew points back\ninto the 60s, generating MUCAPE of 500-1000 J/kg and deep-layer\nshear of 25-30 knots. This environment will bring scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms from Tuesday afternoon through\nWednesday morning. While severe weather risk remains low at this\ntime due to modest instability, parallel flow aloft relative to\nthe frontal boundary could result in localized training of shower\nactivity. Lighter precipitation chances linger into Thursday as\ncooler, near-normal air settles back over the region with highs\nreturning to the upper 70s and lower 80s.",
